### Why Are Fields Not Polymorphic?

In Java, **polymorphism applies exclusively to non-private, non-static instance methods**. Fields are **statically bound at compile time**.

When a subclass declares a field with the exact same name as an inherited field from its superclass:
- It does **NOT** override the field.
- It **hides** the superclass field.
- The heap object actually contains **BOTH** fields in memory simultaneously!

### Which Field Is Accessed?
```java
class Parent {
    public String name = "ParentName";
}
class Child extends Parent {
    public String name = "ChildName"; // Hides Parent.name!
}

Parent p = new Child();
System.out.println(p.name); // Prints "ParentName"! (Resolved by Reference Type!)
Child c = (Child) p;
System.out.println(c.name); // Prints "ChildName"! (Resolved by Child Reference!)
```

### The OOP Best Practice: Encapsulation Prevents Field Hiding
Because field hiding causes immense confusion and subtle bugs, the universal Java rule is:
> **Always mark fields `private`. Expose state exclusively through getter methods.**

Getter methods **are** polymorphic and override cleanly, ensuring consistent, predictable behavior across all references.

activities.codeSnippet:
class SuperHero {
    public String identity = "Secret Identity"; // Field

    public String getVoice() {                 // Method
        return "Normal human voice";
    }
}

class SpiderMan extends SuperHero {
    public String identity = "Peter Parker";    // Hides SuperHero.identity!

    @Override
    public String getVoice() {                  // Overrides getVoice()
        return "Friendly neighborhood Spider-Man!";
    }
}

public class FieldHidingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Reference type: SuperHero, Actual object: SpiderMan
        SuperHero hero = new SpiderMan();

        // 1. Field is resolved by Reference Type (SuperHero) -> "Secret Identity"
        System.out.println("Field access: " + hero.identity);

        // 2. Method is resolved by Runtime Object (SpiderMan) -> "Friendly neighborhood..."
        System.out.println("Method call:  " + hero.getVoice());
    }
}

